Project Artifacts
1. What are Project Artifacts?
- Project artifacts are documents, templates, reports, plans, and deliverables created during a project.
- They help explain and support project activities.
- They help set expectations, meet stakeholder needs, and achieve organizational goals.
- Examples: Project charter, business case, project plans, and progress reports.
Characteristics of Project Artifacts:
1. Generic: Some artifacts can be used in different projects.
2. Industry-specific: Some artifacts are designed for a particular industry.
3. Dynamic: Artifacts can be updated when project changes happen.
4. Method-based: Some artifacts are created using specific methods or techniques.
Important: Project artifacts can be modified and updated throughout the project.
2. Dynamic Nature of Artifacts
- Project artifacts are not always fixed documents.
- They can change according to project progress and environmental changes.
- Example: A monthly progress report is updated every month with new project information.
- This process continues until the project goals are achieved.
3. Project Initiation Stage Artifacts
- During the initiation stage, project goals and requirements are identified.
- Important documents are prepared to start the project.
- These documents include project objectives, budget, schedule, responsibilities, and stakeholders.
- Most documents are prepared at the beginning and are not changed frequently.
Types of Project Initiation Artifacts

1. Strategy Artifacts

These documents explain the main direction and purpose of the project.

Examples:
- Business case
- Scope statement
- Project charter
- Project roadmap
2. Project Plans

These plans explain how different project activities will be managed.

Examples:
- Communication management plan
- Release plan
- Scope management plan
- Iteration plan
- Test plan
- Quality plan
- Logistics plan
All these plans are included in the Project Management Plan. They can be prepared as one document or separate documents.

3. Hierarchy Charts
- These charts show how project components are divided into smaller parts.
- They help organize and understand project information.
Examples:
- Work Breakdown Structure (WBS)
- Product Breakdown Structure (PBS)
- Organizational Breakdown Structure (OBS)
- Risk Breakdown Structure (RBS)
4. Project Baselines
- Baselines are approved versions of project plans.
- They are used to compare actual project performance with the original plan.
Types:
- Cost Baseline – Approved project budget.
- Schedule Baseline – Approved project timeline.
- Scope Baseline – Approved project scope.
- Performance Measurement Baseline – Used to measure overall project performance.
5. Other Artifacts

Other documents and visual materials prepared during project initiation.

Examples:
- Registers: Stakeholder register.
- Visual artifacts: S-curves, flowcharts, project dashboards.
- Reports: Quality reports, risk reports, progress reports.
- Agreements: Fixed-price contracts, cost-reimbursable contracts, time and material contracts.

4. Project Execution Stage Artifacts
- During the execution stage, the project team starts doing the planned activities.
- The project manager monitors team activities and makes sure deadlines are met.
- Documents are updated to show the actual project progress.
Examples of execution stage artifacts:
1. Project tracking workbook – Tracks project progress.
2. Decision log – Records important decisions.
3. Change request form – Records requested project changes.
4. Risk log – Records project risks.
5. Version Controlling
- Version control is the process of managing different versions of a document.
- It keeps track of changes made to documents.
- It helps identify previous and latest versions.
- It provides a record of who changed documents and when.
- It helps avoid confusion when multiple people update the same document.
Example:
- Version 1.0 – Initial project plan.
- Version 1.1 – Updated project plan.
- Version 2.0 – Major changes to the project plan.
Importance of Version Control:
1. Tracks document changes.
2. Maintains previous versions.
3. Avoids confusion.
4. Helps identify the latest approved document.
5. Provides a history of updates.

